const LESSONS=[
 {intro:'Decode genotypes, alleles, and the difference between DNA instructions and observable traits.',body:`<h3>🧬 Welcome to the Gene Lab</h3>
 <p><strong>DNA</strong> contains genetic information. A <strong>gene</strong> is a stretch of DNA that contributes to a functional product or trait. An <strong>allele</strong> is one version of a gene. In the simple diploid models used here, an individual inherits two alleles for each autosomal gene: one from each parent.</p>
 <div class="example-grid"><div><b>BB</b><br>Homozygous dominant: two dominant alleles</div><div><b>Bb</b><br>Heterozygous: two different alleles</div><div><b>bb</b><br>Homozygous recessive: two recessive alleles</div></div>
 <p><strong>Genotype</strong> = allele combination (like Bb). <strong>Phenotype</strong> = observable characteristic (like black eyes in a fictional mouse model).</p>
 <p>In <strong>complete dominance</strong>, one dominant allele is sufficient for the dominant phenotype: BB and Bb have the same phenotype; only bb shows the recessive phenotype.</p>
 <h3>🔑 How to solve any basic genetics question</h3><ul><li>1. Write the parental genotypes.</li><li>2. List the alleles each parent can pass in gametes.</li><li>3. Combine alleles in a Punnett square.</li><li>4. Count offspring <strong>genotypes</strong>.</li><li>5. Translate them into expected <strong>phenotypes</strong> and probabilities.</li></ul>
 <p><strong>Scientific note:</strong> These are useful single-gene teaching models. Human traits such as eye color and hairline shape are generally more complex than a single dominant/recessive gene.</p>`},
 {intro:'Build and solve 2 × 2 Punnett squares, then turn the results into probabilities.',body:`<h3>🌱 The Punnett Square Power-Up</h3>
 <p><strong>Meiosis</strong> produces gametes that usually receive one allele per gene. During fertilization, one gamete from each parent combines to produce an offspring genotype.</p>
 <p>Cross <strong>Bb × bb</strong>. The first parent can make B or b gametes; the second makes b or b. A 2 × 2 Punnett square shows four equally likely combinations:</p>
 <div class="tutorial-sq" aria-label="Punnett square for Bb crossed with bb"><div>×</div><div>B</div><div>b</div><div>b</div><div>Bb</div><div>bb</div><div>b</div><div>Bb</div><div>bb</div></div>
 <p><strong>Genotype probabilities:</strong> 2 of 4 Bb (50%), 2 of 4 bb (50%). If B is completely dominant, the predicted <strong>phenotypic ratio</strong> is 1 dominant : 1 recessive.</p>
 <p>Another classic cross is <strong>Ww × Ww</strong>, producing WW, Ww, Ww, ww. This is a <strong>1:2:1 genotypic ratio</strong> and, under complete dominance, a <strong>3:1 phenotypic ratio</strong>.</p>
 <h3>🎲 Probability is not a promise</h3><p>A 50% probability means an expected 25 out of 50 in a very large collection of similar crosses; an actual set of 50 may differ. Each conception is a new probability event, not a fixed alternating sequence.</p>
 <p>Some allele combinations can affect survival. A predicted percentage at conception is not necessarily the percentage among surviving offspring.</p>`},
 {intro:'Use offspring patterns as clues to identify the genotypes that could have produced them.',body:`<h3>🕵️ Work Backward from the Evidence</h3>
 <p>With <strong>complete dominance</strong>, an organism that displays the dominant phenotype could be <strong>AA</strong> or <strong>Aa</strong>. Its appearance alone does not prove which genotype it has.</p>
 <div class="example-grid"><div><b>Aa × aa</b><br>50% Aa / 50% aa<br>Expected 1:1 phenotype ratio</div><div><b>Aa × Aa</b><br>25% AA / 50% Aa / 25% aa<br>Expected 3:1 ratio</div><div><b>AA × aa</b><br>100% Aa<br>All display the dominant phenotype</div></div>
 <h3>🔎 Run a test cross</h3><p>To test an unknown dominant-looking individual, cross it with a <strong>homozygous recessive (aa)</strong> partner. If any offspring are aa, the unknown parent must carry an a allele, making it Aa.</p>
 <p><strong>Important detective rule:</strong> Seeing only dominant-looking offspring does <em>not</em> prove the unknown is AA, especially with a small sample. In an Aa × aa cross, three consecutive dominant-looking offspring occur with probability (1/2)³ = <strong>1/8</strong>.</p>
 <p>Pay attention to the difference between a logical impossibility (an AA parent cannot pass a) and a low-probability event (a heterozygote can happen to produce several dominant-looking offspring).</p>`},
 {intro:'Go beyond one trait: independent assortment, flower color, and ABO blood group inheritance.',body:`<h3>🌈 Two Genes at Once: Dihybrid Crosses</h3>
 <p>A genotype <strong>RrYy</strong> includes two genes. If they assort independently, its possible gametes are <strong>RY, Ry, rY, ry</strong>. For an RrYy × RrYy cross with simple dominance and independent assortment, the typical phenotypic ratio is <strong>9:3:3:1</strong>.</p>
 <p>For the specific cross <strong>RrYy × rrYy</strong>, texture segregates 1:1 and color 3:1, giving <strong>3 round yellow : 1 round green : 3 wrinkled yellow : 1 wrinkled green</strong>.</p>
 <h3>🌸 Not All Traits Have Complete Dominance</h3>
 <p><strong>Incomplete dominance</strong> produces an intermediate heterozygote phenotype. In the example used in this game: <strong>rr = red</strong>, <strong>ww = white</strong>, and <strong>rw = pink</strong>. Pink × pink yields red : pink : white in a 1:2:1 ratio.</p>
 <p><strong>Codominance</strong> means both alleles are expressed distinctly in the heterozygote. The ABO blood group is a standard example: <strong>Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant</strong>, while <strong>i is recessive</strong> to both.</p>
 <div class="example-grid"><div><b>Type A</b><br>IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi</div><div><b>Type B</b><br>IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi</div><div><b>Type AB</b><br>IᴬIᴮ</div><div><b>Type O</b><br>ii</div></div>
 <p>Crossing AB (IᴬIᴮ) × O (ii) produces half type A (Iᴬi) and half type B (Iᴮi). No type O offspring arise in the standard model.</p>`},
 {intro:'Trace X-linked traits and interpret patterns in a family pedigree.',body:`<h3>🧩 Sex-Linked Inheritance</h3>
 <p>A gene is <strong>X-linked</strong> when it is located on the X chromosome. In the typical XX/XY model, XX individuals have two X chromosomes, while XY individuals have one X and one Y.</p>
 <p>For an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> condition, an XY individual with the recessive allele on their single X typically expresses the trait. A heterozygous XX individual is often called a <strong>carrier</strong> (though biological details vary by condition).</p>
 <p>Example: carrier mother <strong>XᴺXⁿ</strong> × unaffected father <strong>XᴺY</strong>. For sons, half inherit Xᴺ and half inherit Xⁿ: <strong>50% of sons</strong> are expected to express the recessive condition. That's different from 50% of <em>all children</em>.</p>
 <h3>🌳 Read a Pedigree</h3>
 <div class="example-grid"><div><b>□ Square</b><br>Traditionally male in a pedigree</div><div><b>○ Circle</b><br>Traditionally female</div><div><b>■ / ● Shading</b><br>Individual displays the tracked trait</div></div>
 <p>Lines connect parents and children. A recessive trait can appear in a child whose parents do not express it, if both parents are <strong>carriers (Aa × Aa)</strong>.</p>
 <h3>🏁 Final Challenge Advice</h3><p>For ABO inheritance, type O requires <strong>ii</strong>—an i allele from <em>both</em> parents. A standard type AB individual is IᴬIᴮ and has no i to pass. Real genetic counseling and parentage testing involve far more than a single classroom Punnett square.</p>`}
];
